; Jsont for Melange. The patch fixes two independent things, one a compile
; error and one a runtime crash.
;
; 1. jsont.ml declares BOTH `exception Error of error` and `module Error`.
;    Melange cannot emit a unit exporting one name twice and rejects the file:
;
;      Error: Error are exported as twice
;
;    Not a version to upgrade past -- melange 5.1.0-414 and 7.0.1-414 both
;    refuse it, and it reproduces in a four-line module. The melange copy
;    renames the exception to [Exn]. Nothing here is affected: the only site
;    catching it is test/native4d/shape4_test.ml, which is native-only.
;
;    A recorded patch, not a regex. `Error` appears in three namespaces --
;    exception constructor, module, result constructor -- textually identical.
;    jsont.ml:2058 is `| Error e ->` on a *result*; a substitution over that
;    form corrupts it and still compiles. The patch was made by renaming the
;    unambiguous sites, then letting the compiler name the remaining eight
;    exception-position arms one at a time. That is the only way to tell them
;    apart, and the sha256 guard is what makes it safe to rely on afterwards.
;
; 2. `let rec elt = Repr.Rec any and ... and any = lazy ...` (jsont.ml:1727) is
;    a recursive value definition. Melange emits it as a Caml_obj.update_dummy
;    over a `const` declared later in the file, and JS's temporal dead zone
;    makes that a hard runtime failure the moment anything requires jsont:
;
;      ReferenceError: Cannot access 'any$1' before initialization
;
;    It COMPILES cleanly, so only running the output catches it -- which is the
;    argument for melange.runtest existing at all rather than stopping at a
;    build check. Wrapping the reference in `lazy (Lazy.force any)` defers it
;    past initialisation. Semantically a no-op: `Rec` is consumed through
;    `Lazy.force`, so the extra lazy forces to the same value.
;
;    THIS HUNK IS A BACKPORT, not a permanent workaround. It is a melange
;    codegen bug, fixed upstream by melange-re/melange#1735 and released in
;    melange 7.0.0. Verified: on 7.0.1-414 the Error rename alone produces JS
;    that runs and matches the native golden exactly. Drop this hunk when the
;    pin can move to >= 7.0.0 -- which needs OCaml >= 5.1, see
;    .ai/js_backends_design.md. Hunk 1 stays either way; 7.0.1-414 still
;    rejects the duplicate export.

(rule
 (target jsont.ml)
 (enabled_if
  (= %{profile} melange))
 (deps patch.sh jsont.ml.patch)
 (action
  (run
   bash
   patch.sh
   %{lib:jsont:jsont.ml}
   ab7808a381224bdf8a7d74967c79f65db40febb555871b651f37fde01495e112
   jsont.ml.patch
   %{target})))

(rule
 (target jsont.mli)
 (enabled_if
  (= %{profile} melange))
 (deps patch.sh jsont.mli.patch)
 (action
  (run
   bash
   patch.sh
   %{lib:jsont:jsont.mli}
   ba0ffff8f5319584ed08bf9ec051cbbd622e9bbb763f3014dacc75a6c444a60a
   jsont.mli.patch
   %{target})))

(library
 (name jsont_mel)
 (wrapped false)
 (modes melange)
 (enabled_if
  (= %{profile} melange))
 (libraries bigarray_mel jsont_base_mel)
 (flags
  (:standard -w -a)))
